1
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:05,800
Writing one good sales email takes me about twenty minutes.

2
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:09,670
Last week, I wrote 111 of them in one evening.

3
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:11,170
Each one personal.

4
00:00:11,410 --> 00:00:12,070
Here's how.

5
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:14,370
Hi, I'm Jefferson from CoLab.

6
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:19,250
We teach airline reservations and hotel systems to college students.

7
00:00:19,490 --> 00:00:21,950
Most sales emails sound exactly the same.

8
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:24,940
"Dear Sir or Madam, we offer training."

9
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,460
A dean gets dozens of those every week.

10
00:00:27,710 --> 00:00:29,790
If an email doesn't name their school,

11
00:00:29,950 --> 00:00:31,550
and say why it fits their students,

12
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:32,740
it gets deleted.

13
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:36,990
But writing a personal email for every school takes hours.

14
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:38,760
So I gave that job to Claude.

15
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,180
Here's the secret:

16
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:43,120
the email is only as good as what you tell the AI.

17
00:00:43,330 --> 00:00:46,520
Before it writes anything, Claude needs four things.

18
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:50,330
One: who I am, and what I sell, in plain words.

19
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:53,100
Two: my offer in three short points,

20
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:54,810
the price, and what I want them to do.

21
00:00:55,070 --> 00:00:58,790
For me, that's a fifteen-minute online presentation.

22
00:00:58,810 --> 00:01:01,990
Three: my proposal PDF, to attach.

23
00:01:02,270 --> 00:01:06,290
And four: my lead sheet, with every school's name,

24
00:01:06,310 --> 00:01:09,600
official email, department and the programs it offers.

25
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:11,800
I wrote that once, in one prompt.

26
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:14,670
After that, it works for every school.

27
00:01:14,930 --> 00:01:17,200
So what makes each email personal?

28
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:19,320
The subject line has the school's name.

29
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:23,780
The email starts by asking the office to forward it to the dean

30
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:26,520
of their Tourism and Hospitality programs.

31
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:30,200
If the school publishes the dean's name on its official website,

32
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:32,230
it adds an Attention line with that name.

33
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:36,220
Then it only talks about the programs that school actually offers.

34
00:01:36,470 --> 00:01:39,130
Tourism only? Just the airline course.

35
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:42,640
Both? Both courses, and both PDFs.

36
00:01:42,870 --> 00:01:46,890
And it checks my Google Calendar and offers two real free times

37
00:01:46,910 --> 00:01:47,860
for the presentation.

38
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:52,600
Same structure every time, but every email fits that school.

39
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:54,000
Here's the important part.

40
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:55,750
Every one of these is a draft.

41
00:01:55,770 --> 00:01:57,350
Nothing is sent.

42
00:01:57,370 --> 00:01:59,600
They sit in my Gmail drafts folder,

43
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:00,580
waiting for me.

44
00:02:00,810 --> 00:02:04,540
And before it writes a new email, Claude searches my Sent folder.

45
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:06,440
If I've already emailed that school,

46
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:08,640
it does not start a new email.

47
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:10,960
It writes a short follow-up in the same thread.

48
00:02:11,190 --> 00:02:13,590
So no dean gets the same proposal twice.

49
00:02:13,610 --> 00:02:17,270
That check alone saves you from looking careless in front of

50
00:02:17,290 --> 00:02:18,500
a school you want to work with.

51
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:22,350
Before anything goes out, I open five drafts and read them like

52
00:02:22,370 --> 00:02:23,290
the dean would.

53
00:02:23,310 --> 00:02:24,850
Is the school's name right?

54
00:02:24,870 --> 00:02:26,360
Is the right course in it?

55
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:27,900
Is the PDF attached?

56
00:02:28,170 --> 00:02:31,150
If something's off, I don't fix each email by hand.

57
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:34,240
I fix the prompt, and let Claude make the drafts again.

58
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:36,320
Two things I learned the hard way.

59
00:02:36,430 --> 00:02:40,200
One: if you edit a draft, the attachment can disappear.

60
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:41,940
Make a new draft instead.

61
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:46,350
Two: before sending, ask Claude to list the drafts by school,

62
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:47,830
so nobody gets two.

63
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:50,640
111 drafts. One evening.

64
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:53,020
And I read them before a single one went out.

65
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:56,790
Once I'm happy, I don't hit send on all 111.

66
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:59,940
Send that many at once, and Gmail thinks you're a spammer.

67
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:03,660
Instead, a scheduled task sends 25 at a time,

68
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:07,420
one batch per hour, from 8:30 to 12:30.

69
00:03:07,650 --> 00:03:10,640
It only sends drafts with my approved subject lines,

70
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:13,640
so nothing else in my drafts folder goes out by accident.

71
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:17,490
And after each batch, it marks those schools "Proposal Sent"

72
00:03:17,510 --> 00:03:18,810
in my lead sheet.

73
00:03:18,830 --> 00:03:21,680
And then, about twenty minutes after the first batch,

74
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:23,050
a reply came in.

75
00:03:23,270 --> 00:03:26,380
A university's department head had forwarded my proposal to their

76
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:29,730
program dean and staff, asked them to look at the fit and the

77
00:03:29,750 --> 00:03:32,880
fee, and to pick one of the two dates I offered.

78
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:34,800
That's what a personal email does.

79
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:36,000
It doesn't just get read.

80
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:37,400
It gets passed on.

81
00:03:37,650 --> 00:03:38,480
So remember the rule:

82
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:41,100
Claude prepares. I approve.

83
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:42,250
Then it sends.

84
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:46,040
The AI writes the drafts, but I'm still the one who decides what

85
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:48,690
goes out. If you want to do this for your business,

86
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:51,330
every prompt from this video is in my playbook.

87
00:03:51,350 --> 00:03:53,430
The link is in the description.

88
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:54,510
Not ready yet?

89
00:03:54,710 --> 00:03:57,630
There's a free starter pack with 10 of my sales prompts,

90
00:03:57,880 --> 00:03:59,060
also linked below.

91
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:02,050
And subscribe, because next week I'll show you what happens when

92
00:04:02,070 --> 00:04:03,250
the replies come in:

93
00:04:03,510 --> 00:04:06,350
how my AI reads them, checks my calendar,

94
00:04:06,370 --> 00:04:09,320
and drafts the answer with real free time slots.

95
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:10,350
See you then.
